    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the training part sounds a bit difficult since some of our senior partners aren't great with tech. how do i explain the \"threat\" part to them without making it sound like we're being paranoid? like, what are we actually protecting against in a way that makes sense to them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can explain the threat to your senior partners by framing it as a \"digital thief\" who has already bypassed the front door (your password) and is waiting patiently for a simple text message to slip through the cracks. Instead of talking about complex hacking vectors, tell them that SMS MFA is like giving a stranger a spare key to your office because they found a note on the front porch.\n\nTo make this relatable without sounding paranoid, use a \"goblin\" analogy: Imagine a goblin who has already stolen your gold (your password) and is now sitting in the corner of the room, waiting for you to hand over the final coin (the SMS code) so he can take everything. The goblin doesn't care about your fancy locks; he just wants that one easy coin. By switching to a YubiKey, you are essentially telling the goblin, \"I don't know you, and I won't give you the coin unless you show me your physical ID card right now.\"\n\nFor the rollout, start with a \"goblin audit\" where you show them exactly how easy it is for a hacker to steal their data via SMS, then introduce the YubiKey as the \"goblin-proof\" shield",
      "source": "target",
